<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cao Huan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">246–302</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Emperor Yuan of Wei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cao Huan was the last emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>, reigning from 260 to 266 as a figurehead under the Sima family. During his reign Wei conquered Shu Han in 263. In 266 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-jin">Sima Yan</a> forced him to abdicate and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-jin-dynasty">Western Jin dynasty</a>, granting Cao Huan the title Prince of Chenliu, which he held until his death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cao Huan was born with the name Cao Huang. His father, Cao Yu, the Prince of Yan, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, the father of Wei's first emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi">Cao Pi</a>. In 258, at the age of 12, Cao Huan was instated as the Duke of Changdao District.</p><p>In June 260 the emperor Cao Mao was killed in an attempt to seize back state power from the regent Sima Zhao. Cao Huang was selected to succeed him. His name was then changed to Cao Huan, because the old name was a homonym of many common words, including those for yellow and for emperor, and so hard to avoid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>During his reign the Sima clan controlled the state, and Cao Huan was a figurehead and head of state in name only. In the first years of the reign the rival state of Shu Han, under the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-wei">Jiang Wei</a>, attacked repeatedly, though these attacks were largely repelled.</p><p>Sima Zhao then ordered a counterattack on Shu with 180,000 men under Zhong Hui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-ai">Deng Ai</a>. In late 263 the Shu emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shan">Liu Shan</a> surrendered to Deng Ai, ending the state of Shu. In early 264 Zhong Hui plotted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-wei">Jiang Wei</a> to restore Shu, and the Wei generals killed both men. Shu's former lands were annexed by Wei.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In December 263 Sima Zhao again pressed Cao Huan to grant him the nine bestowments, and this time accepted them, which signalled that a usurpation was near. In May 264 he became King of Jin. After he died in September 265, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-jin">Sima Yan</a> inherited his position and on 4 February 266 forced Cao Huan to abdicate. Cao Huan was sent to the city of Ye, where he lived for the rest of his life, and Sima Yan founded the Jin dynasty on 8 February.</p><p>Little is known of Cao Huan's life as a prince. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-jin">Sima Yan</a> allowed him to keep imperial banners and wagons and to worship his ancestors with imperial ceremonies. He died in the reign of Emperor Hui, was buried with the honours due an emperor and was given the posthumous name Yuan. The title of Prince of Chenliu stayed within the Cao clan until it was abolished in 479.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cao Huan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wei conquered Shu Han in 263, and in 266 Sima Yan forced <b>Cao Huan</b> to abdicate, founding the Western Jin.</blockquote><a href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a></li></ul></section>
